ype of the  line  and several labs uh also uh uh prefer to  do  whole genome sequencing so you can  detect all your deletions insertions uh  somatic uh  uh somatic uh sorry single nuclear  variants and the nobel variants etc  okay so one of the key challenges in the  ips field is a phenomena  called interline variability basically  means that if you take a population of  donor cells and you derive  two ips lines in the same reprogramming  experiment to derive two  independent ips clones or ips lines from  this donor population  and differentiate both of these lines  using the same protocol the lines might  actually  behave slightly different from each  other in terms of their  differentiation potential and lineage  bias  uh so now why is that although they were  both derived  from the same population of donor cells  in the same reprogramming experiment  so there are there could be many causes  for interline variability uh  the two main causes that i'll talk about  here one is  if you have a heterogeneous donor  population as i mentioned earlier every  ips clone ips line comes from the  reprogramming of a single donor cell as  a result of a single reprogramming event  so if you have a heterogeneous  population of donor cells  which are where the different cells  carry different cohorts of some  somatic mutations those mutations  basically are going to get transferred  over to the ips lines that come from  these cells  and resulting in a heterogeneous group  of ips  lines derived from the same donor  population the second reason  um could be aberrant epigenetic  resetting so what is epigenetic  resetting  now during reprogramming um you start  with a donor somatic cell  where this is a fully differentiated  cell so all the pluripotency genes  are repressed which means they carry uh  hypermethylated promoters and  repressive histone modifications such as  the lysine  9 and lysine 27 trimethylation whereas  all the cell type specific genes are  active  so they have hypo