s to determine how  closely does this organoid resemble  the region of the brain that you're  trying to model or mimic  now characterizing these models uh again  involves three main aspects there are  several assays that you can do for  characterizing your orders but the three  main aspects of it  the first one is the immunostaining  which is basically which involves simply  sectioning the organoids and staining  them  for um sustaining them using antibodies  against specific cell type  markers cell type specific markers and  genes here for example we have a bunch  of  rostral hind brain organoids that we  made in the lab and we've stained them  sectioned and stained them in  collaboration with the northcott lab  to look for the presence of to check the  presence of a certain specific cell type  so  uh immunostaining is the advantage of a  quick immunostaining is first of all  it's a good way to determine if you are  going in the right direction if you are  different if you are differentiating the  right  lineages just to confirm the presence of  specific cell types that you expect to  see in the brain region that you're  trying to model  it also shows you cellular organization  and cyto architecture within the  organoid itself if the cells are  layered uh they're laminated and the  distribution and localization of the  cell of the different cell types  relative to each other so it gives you  um  it gives you uh an idea of how well  the organoids the cells are organized  within the organoid structure  um and is it similar to what you would  uh see in vivo  uh the second aspect of characterization  is by is looking at the transcription  profile uh so you want  you could do a bunch of rtq pcrs or you  can do bulk rna-seq or more commonly  what's becoming more and more common in  the field  is to do a single cell rna feed and here  are some examples of single salary speak  data published  by different labs uh the um  the advantage is that this basically uh  the essay tells you  uh what proportion of a cell types of  different cell types are present in your  organoid  uh if you have the and it also tells you  if you have uh  cell types that you do not want in your  organized system so  so cells from the neighboring lineages  or neighboring parts of the brain  the undesirable cell types so um  so this is a more quantitative analysis  of your organ  of the composition of your organoid  and the third uh aspect of  characterization is to perform a  functional as you know because we have a  brain organoids  so we are our the functional assay is  essentially the  the best way to uh sort of demonstrate  the physiological relevance of your  organoid model  so for brain organoids we want to know  if they have a functional neurons if  they have a neurons that can actually  form a neural network and function  and and fire spontaneously uh so  in our then again there are different  ways to do this but in our lab we  typically uh plate organoids on these  multi-electrode arrays uh we have a  system from axion  biosystems uh here we have a grid of 16  electrodes  and there's a critical organoid that we  have plated on these  the multi-electrode array essentially  measures  extracellular field potential uh which  is  uh which is a direct consequence of the  firing of action potentials uh  action potential by the neurons which  are which are in contact with these  electrodes  now if you when you have a in cortical  organoids when you have  um a mature cortical organoid of  these neurons which with a dense network  of neurons  they will basically communicate with  each other and fire synchronously  um as shown here uh where you can  so basically each of these uh raster  plots is from a separate organoid  and on the x-axis is the time and on  y-axis is the 16 different electrodes  basically each line each vertical line  here represents  um the firing of an action potential so  as you can see here  you have a bunch of electrodes firing um  uh synchronously you're fighting firing  um  at the same time which basically  suggests that there is a network of  neurons across  these 16 electrodes and um and this is  this is what you would expect in a  hundred day old cortical organoids  um notice here that each of these  organoids has a slightly different  pattern of firing but they all  are able to fire uh these neuronal uh  these network bursting or network  oscillations  okay uh so um so a challenge  just like ips lines uh the the challenge  in in the organoid field is variability  amongst organoids  now um organized generated within uh the  same  experiment within the same batch as well  as organized between  independent experiments using the exact  same protocol can be quite variable  uh we have seen variability in yield  we've seen variability in size for  example here we have a bunch of  cortical organoids which are highly  variable in size we've seen variability  in the morphology of the organoids  so um here we have a batch which has a  bunch of  a good looking dense um organoids with  smooth translucent edges but we also  have a ton of cystic organoids  um we here is a reporter line um a bunch  of cortical organoids expressing a  reporter now we have some organizers  expressing the reporter very  um very efficiently and strongly and we  have a bunch of organoids  with no reported expression this is  within the same batch then here we have  a bunch of caudal forebrain organoids um  expressing a different reporter again  you can see there's some organized with  strong full expression of reporters some  with weak expression  some without any expression at all and  some uh expressing the reporter in a  in patches throughout the organized so  the variability can be quite  um quite significant so what is the  reason for this variability  um there there could be many reasons but  the two reasons that we are focusing on  resolving in the lab are um first um  uh if you start with a heterogeneous a  population of ipsc