ond 80 percent confluency for example  uh and just  passaging them and maintaining them the  craft preservation just optimal handling  conditions of the ips lines  also helps in maintaining uh the  maintaining a healthy line and therefore  reducing availability downstream  um and in order to address the the  efficiency issue of the differentiation  protocol that you're using uh  my recommendation would be to use a  reporter line wherever possible  now we've had issues with some of the  protocols that we we've tried to develop  to model different  parts of the brain and uh using a  reporter line is very is extremely  helpful  in uh optimizing your protocol uh in in  in determining the variability in your  protocol the  variability in your system in  determining the  efficiency of your system so wherever  possible use a reporter line and then we  can also use these uh  reporter lines to essentially screen  organoids at an early stage  uh screen organized that are expressing  the reporters versus organized that  don't express a reporter well for  example and before before uh  uh before doing the more um uh  detailed experiments or downstream  analysis at an early point during  differentiation you could use the  reporter line to screen the healthy  organoids so the good organoids  so i'll give you an example here so here  we have a culture  a batch of caudal forebrain organized  expressing a  cardinal four brain specific reporter  now um  you can see this argonaut is a mix of  this this population is a mix of  organized expressing the reporter  marked here with the yellow asterisk  aspect and then we also have organized  uh marked here with the blue  asterisk which don't express the  reporter at all and we have we have all  of these argonauts growing in a single  batch  so now what we did was we basically  compared the  gene expression profile by bulk rna-seq  of uh organoids which were made from a  line from a non-reported line so we  basically collected a bunch of  unscreened organoids  and then we made organoids using the  same protocol from the reporter line  and we screened for the reporter  expressing organized and then uh  did a bulk rna-seq on those organoids  now in this heat map which is a subset  of the rna-seq data  we are looking at the expression of  caudal forebrain genes and dorsal for  brain genes and each row here represents  a single organoid one organoid basically  and as you can see in the reporter's  screen if you look at the reporter  screen organized they almost all look  almost exactly similar with a high  expression of  caudal forebrain genes and low  expression of dorsal for brain genes but  if you look at the unscreened organoids  not only do they have a completely  different expression profile  they also their organelles also differ  from each other  quite a bit in terms of the level of  expression of the different genes  so um if whenever possible use a  reporter line and use that to screen  organoids before you start doing the  more uh  the the bigger downstream experiments  um okay so now i want  to uh give you uh two examples of  disease modeling using ipscs and  ipsc-derived organoids that we have done  in the lab in collaboration with pis in  the developmental neurobiology  department at century  the first uh example is a project we did  in collaboration with dr mike dyer  um uh where where the goal was to model  human retinoblastoma in order to  identify the cell of origin of this  tumor  and uh to provide a model for  pre-clinical testing  now the uh the uh our role  or our job in this collaboration was to  uh develop and optimize a proto a highly  reliable and efficient protocol  that could differentiate any ips line  um into a retinal organoid and which is  basically what we  worked on uh we we used a couple of  reporter lines here which  the dyer lab received as gifts from  their collaborators  and using these reporter lines we  basically optimized  a protocol um that was that where  whereby we could uh differentiate  ipscs from 15 different individuals  efficiently into retinal organoids with  a good yield  and a decent morphology  we also saw that our retinal organoids  they are  are functional so um  we have we have we have  we've seen retinal pigment epithelial  cells juxtaposed  with photoreceptors which basically  suggest the formation of a functional  photoreceptor signaling  and we've also seen that older retinal  organoids when we played them on a  multi-electrode array and provide them  light stimulus  we were able to record a wavelength  specific life response from the retinal  ganglion cells  in these organoids which basically  suggest a functional  a pathway between photoreceptors and  retinal ganglion so we have functional  retinal organoids  that we could derive from multiple  individuals so once we had the protocol  um we worked very closely with jackie  who's an excellent postdoc in the dyer  lab  so um what jackie basically did was um  jackie and her other collaborators they  basically collected  um uh donor cells from  15 different retinoblastoma patients uh  carrying  a different a wide-ranging  mutants uh sorry mutations in the in the  rb1  locus uh they then um generated ips  lines from these  15 different individuals we then  screened the ips clones and we selected  the clones  we did a neural reset formation essay  and we selected the clones that had high  neurogenic potential  and we were then able to differentiate  ips lines from each of the 15 patients  into retinal organoids  jackie then took these organoids and she  dissociated on at 45 days of  differentiation dissociated them into  single cells and injected them  into a materially into immunocompromised  mice  and after about a year she was able to  see that  seven out of the 15 organoids 15  patients specific organoids were able to  generate a retinoblastoma  which is a tumor of the eye in these  mice  and we could uh they could they could  basically confirm this by histology  um and they also looked at the dna  methylation profile in these  um in these organoid derived  retinoblastomas and they found that  uh the organoid tumors they clustered  with the patient's tumors and the  xenografts  uh they also did single cell rna-seq on  these uh on these retinoblastomas and  found that the organoid derived tumors  again they look remarkably similar to  the  patient derived tumors as well as the  patient derived xenograft  with regard to cell type identity and  proliferation  and this work is we're about to publish  this work  it's in the final stages of submission  um in the second example we worked in  collaboration with  dr fabio de montes the de montes lab  studies aging and  degeneration in drosophila as their  modal system but what they wanted to  know was  they wanted to see if stress induced  neuro degeneration is something that can  be modeled  in a in a human organoid system  so uh the demontis lab basically working  with drosophila they identified a unique  pathway they found that  if you apply proteosomal stress to  skeletal muscle in drosophila  the muscle releases a myokine called  amira  which is able to induce a  neuroprotective effect in brit in in the  retina and brain of the drosophila  so for example they found that  drosophila that expressed a pathogenic  form of the huntington protein they have  these  protein aggregates in the eye however if  you over express amiral  in the skeletal muscle of these  drosophila these aggregates disappear  similarly drosophila that express a  mutant form of the tau protein they  have um they have degenerated  photoreceptors because of this a protein  aggregation  however if you over express amiral in  the skeletal muscles then this  degeneration again  can be rectified so uh they looked into  this pathway a little bit more and they  they found that uh the this effect of uh  this neuroprotective effect of amira  is mediated by our maltose and so the  demonitis lab wanted to know if  this uh pathway um which is active in  drosophila is is it conserved  in humans is is this is this something  that that can be  seen in in a human in the human brain uh  as in in a human system as well so for  that we um  we generated the cortical organoids and  we decided to test  this pathway in um in cortical organoids  by looking at uh heat induced  neurodegeneration now the lab did a  bunch of essays but there's one essay  that i'm going to  highlight here which we did in my lab so  we basically took  uh mature cortical organoids these are  two organoids one treated  control one is a control and the other  was treated with maltose  which which they show which the lab  showed in drosophila has this  neuroprotective effect  um and then we used heat shock as  a means to induce neurodegeneration now  before heat shock  both of these organoids have a strong  spontaneous network activity  uh after seven hours of heat shock uh  maltose treated organoids continued to  have a  strong activity but activity completely  disappeared in the control organized  because of the degeneration induced by  the heat shock and even after 18 hours  of following 18 hours of recovery  following the heat shock following the  end of the heat shock  uh the maltose treated organoids still  have strong spontaneous network activity  whereas the control organoids  barely recovered any activity at all  uh so this uh we recently published this  work in cell metabolism so this  basically and this is a good example of  how  um uh human brain organoids can be used  to validate  findings uh from uh from a different  model system in this case  drosophila which i think is uh pretty  cool  so to summarize this part of my talk i  basically showed you  that ipscs can be differentiated into  these self-organizing 3d structures that  mimic parts of the brain  called organoids and these can be  depending on the protocol that you  design and develop these can be region  specific  however variability amongst organized is  a problem and using a reporter line and  screening organoids  um for specific endpoints or for  specific epidemic features  is very useful to address this  variability  um to characterize brain organized the  different assays can be done but the the  three sort of fundamental aspects of  characterization  involve immunostaining to look at the  cyto architecture within the organoid  transcriptome analysis to look at the  proportion of different cell types  that are present within the organoid and  how pure the organized organoid is  whether it's contaminated by lineages  from neighboring regions of the brain  and by doing a functional assay which  essentially is to um  uh show its physiological relevance  um i showed you that we we in in our  department we've used human ipc's to  model to generate patient-specific  retinoblastoma patient-specific retinal  organoids which were able to generate a  patient's specific latino blastoma and  immunocompromised mice  and these organoid derived  retinoblastomas have um  the same molecular profile as patient  tumors  i also showed you that we've also in our  department have been able to use  ipscs to model stress-induced  neurodegeneration  by looking at the neuronal activity  spontaneous and neuronal network  activity in mature cortical organoids in  response  to a thermal stress and in response to  treatment with and without  neuroprotective factors  that were discovered in the lab um so  basically by  in this project we were able to  translate findings from drosophila to  humans  which i think is pretty exciting  uh and finally i want to thank kyle and  priyanka  who are members of my lab um  who are very hardworking excellent  brilliant people that are very i was  very fortunate to  hire i want to thank all the members  of of my department that we work very  closely with that we collaborate with  um members of the dyer lab zacharenko  northcot and demontes lab  the dnb operation support team  the cytogenetics co