entire orbital shaker is  basically inside the incubator and there  are several that you can purchase which  are small compact and they don't really  buy  they don't cause any vibration you can  just put them inside the incubator and  move your organoids around that also  helps a lot  um with the growth of the organoids  uh the longer the organoids grow in  culture the more necrosis they will  start to  get in the center so all organoids will  have a necrotic core they'll have a  necrotic center because the nutrients  and gas they just cannot reach the  center of the organoid  that is unavoidable so one thing you can  do to  reduce the size of necrosis is cut the  organoids into smaller pieces  periodically so  after a certain age let's say after like  day 35 or 14  every uh one or two weeks we'll take the  organoids and we'll cut them into  smaller pieces usually into in  in half or in like three pieces and then  put them back in the bioreactor and  continue  cutting the organoids into pieces every  now and then that really helps with  controlling the size of the necrosis in  the center of the organoid and keeping  the organoid growing  uh long term and the last thing i would  say is uh  changing a media uh frequently i mean i  know media is expensive and we don't  want to waste media  but we've noticed that if you change the  entire changing half the media if you  change the entire media in the culture  and do it every let's say four or five  days uh that's very very helpful in  keeping the  culture healthy  thank you so much and it looks like we  have time for one more question  so this audience member was wondering if  you could speak a little bit more to the  variability in psc line in terms of  working with them  for example like handling with them  so uh the the ipsc lines themselves  um when you start working with different  ipsc lines you notice that  uh they can have the the the mo the  the undifferentiated uh pr ips line  itself can have like different  m